1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
In a desert in northern Chile, Europe is

2
00:00:02,630 --> 00:00:05,660
building the largest
optical telescope ever made.

3
00:00:06,304 --> 00:00:09,404
Its main mirror will be
thirty-nine metres across.

4
00:00:09,764 --> 00:00:14,004
Stand it on its edge, and it would be
taller than a twelve-storey building.

5
00:00:14,415 --> 00:00:15,935
And it won't be alone.

6
00:00:16,655 --> 00:00:18,835
Europe already runs some of
the world's most powerful

7
00:00:19,065 --> 00:00:22,895
telescopes there, more than ten
thousand kilometres from home.

8
00:00:23,495 --> 00:00:27,635
The organisation behind them is called
the European Southern Observatory.

9
00:00:28,695 --> 00:00:32,515
And that one word, Southern, turns
out to matter more than it seems.

10
00:00:33,899 --> 00:00:35,979
But first, the obvious problem.

11
00:00:36,619 --> 00:00:39,719
The hardest part of a telescope's
job isn't the distance.

12
00:00:40,419 --> 00:00:42,119
It's the last few kilometres.

13
00:00:43,045 --> 00:00:45,345
Light from a distant galaxy can travel for

14
00:00:45,575 --> 00:00:48,285
billions of years with
almost nothing in its way.

15
00:00:48,925 --> 00:00:53,665
Then, in the last thousandth of a
second, it passes through our atmosphere.

16
00:00:54,235 --> 00:00:56,015
And the air is never still.

17
00:00:56,715 --> 00:00:58,675
Pockets of warmer and
cooler air bend the light

18
00:00:58,905 --> 00:01:01,315
slightly differently, many times a second.

19
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:03,555
That's why stars twinkle.

20
00:01:04,169 --> 00:01:05,769
Twinkling looks lovely.

21
00:01:06,389 --> 00:01:10,469
But for an astronomer, it smears a sharp
point of light into a trembling blur.

22
00:01:10,979 --> 00:01:15,119
Then there's water vapour, which
swallows whole bands of infrared light.

23
00:01:15,499 --> 00:01:19,499
And clouds and city lights, which
can end a night before it starts.

24
00:01:20,229 --> 00:01:23,329
So before you ask how big a
telescope should be, you have

25
00:01:23,559 --> 00:01:26,469
to ask where on Earth the sky
gets in the way the least.

26
00:01:27,531 --> 00:01:29,691
One of the best answers
is the Atacama Desert

27
00:01:29,921 --> 00:01:33,251
in Chile, among the
driest places on Earth.

28
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:38,580
The air above it holds very little
water vapour. Clouds are rare.

29
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:40,460
Cities are far away.

30
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:44,920
And the mountains lift the telescopes
above a large part of the atmosphere.

31
00:01:45,754 --> 00:01:50,454
ESO's Paranal Observatory stands
two thousand six hundred metres up.

32
00:01:50,994 --> 00:01:54,334
More than a quarter of the
atmosphere is already below it.

33
00:01:55,255 --> 00:01:58,735
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

34
00:01:59,355 --> 00:02:00,975
It really helps the channel.

35
00:02:03,345 --> 00:02:05,105
So that explains the desert.

36
00:02:05,545 --> 00:02:09,885
But it doesn't explain the
word in ESO's name. Southern.

37
00:02:10,549 --> 00:02:12,789
There are excellent
observing sites much closer

38
00:02:13,019 --> 00:02:15,949
to home, on the Canary
Islands, for example.

39
00:02:16,449 --> 00:02:19,249
European astronomers use them too.

40
00:02:19,641 --> 00:02:22,561
But they share one limitation
with every observatory in Europe.

41
00:02:22,791 --> 00:02:25,561
They're in the northern hemisphere.

42
00:02:26,481 --> 00:02:27,781
Earth is a ball.

43
00:02:28,581 --> 00:02:31,501
From the north, part of the sky is
always hidden below the horizon.

44
00:02:31,731 --> 00:02:35,181
And the hidden part isn't just any part.

45
00:02:35,861 --> 00:02:39,321
The centre of our own galaxy
lies in the southern sky.

46
00:02:40,181 --> 00:02:42,761
From central Europe, it
never gets far above the

47
00:02:42,991 --> 00:02:45,981
horizon, where its light
has to cross the most air.

48
00:02:46,583 --> 00:02:49,363
From Paranal, it passes
almost straight overhead.

49
00:02:50,113 --> 00:02:52,853
The Magellanic Clouds, two
small companion galaxies

50
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:56,113
of our own, can't be
seen from Europe at all.

51
00:02:56,733 --> 00:03:01,013
Neither can Alpha Centauri, the
nearest star system to the Sun.

52
00:03:01,603 --> 00:03:06,603
And in the 1950s, when ESO was first
being planned, every telescope in the

53
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:10,523
world with a mirror two metres or
wider stood in the northern hemisphere.

54
00:03:11,883 --> 00:03:14,163
The southern sky had nothing as big.

55
00:03:14,821 --> 00:03:16,981
That's what the word Southern means.

56
00:03:17,421 --> 00:03:19,941
Chile gives Europe a part
of the sky it can't see from

57
00:03:20,171 --> 00:03:23,141
home, through some of the
clearest air on the planet.

58
00:03:24,079 --> 00:03:28,139
And it paid off. For decades,
telescopes in Chile followed

59
00:03:28,369 --> 00:03:30,999
stars racing around the
centre of our galaxy.

60
00:03:31,815 --> 00:03:35,175
They were orbiting something
dark and enormously heavy: a

61
00:03:35,405 --> 00:03:38,555
black hole about four million
times the mass of the Sun.

62
00:03:38,964 --> 00:03:42,724
That work won a share of the
2020 Nobel Prize in Physics.

63
00:03:45,724 --> 00:03:49,944
But why is this Europe, together,
and not France or Germany on its own?

64
00:03:50,471 --> 00:03:52,351
Because telescopes like these cost more

65
00:03:52,581 --> 00:03:55,051
than most countries
would ever spend alone.

66
00:03:55,791 --> 00:03:59,611
So they share the cost, the
engineers, and the observing time.

67
00:04:00,254 --> 00:04:04,754
ESO was founded in 1962 by
five countries: Belgium,

68
00:04:04,834 --> 00:04:07,514
France, Germany, the
Netherlands and Sweden.

69
00:04:08,134 --> 00:04:11,954
Today it has sixteen member
states, with Chile as the host.

70
00:04:12,661 --> 00:04:16,481
Their first observatory was
La Silla, chosen in 1964.

71
00:04:17,101 --> 00:04:20,341
Then came Paranal, and
the Very Large Telescope.

72
00:04:21,122 --> 00:04:25,442
Despite the name, the VLT
isn't one telescope. It's four.

73
00:04:25,522 --> 00:04:28,642
Each with a mirror eight
point two metres across.

74
00:04:31,222 --> 00:04:33,342
So what does all of this actually see?

75
00:04:33,822 --> 00:04:37,102
Here are two examples, and they
work in very different ways.

76
00:04:37,848 --> 00:04:41,308
The first is an instrument
on the VLT, called ESPRESSO.

77
00:04:42,088 --> 00:04:43,908
It doesn't take pictures of planets.

78
00:04:44,548 --> 00:04:46,168
It watches stars move.

79
00:04:46,865 --> 00:04:50,465
When a planet orbits a star,
the star isn't perfectly still.

80
00:04:51,165 --> 00:04:55,465
Both circle a shared centre of mass,
so the star sways back and forth.

81
00:04:56,094 --> 00:04:59,314
As it moves towards us, its
light shifts slightly bluer.

82
00:04:59,834 --> 00:05:01,974
As it moves away, slightly redder.

83
00:05:02,454 --> 00:05:04,334
ESPRESSO measures that shift.

84
00:05:05,069 --> 00:05:08,229
Take Proxima Centauri, the
nearest star to the Sun.

85
00:05:08,909 --> 00:05:10,629
From Europe, it never rises.

86
00:05:11,396 --> 00:05:15,056
It has a planet, Proxima b,
discovered in 2016 with

87
00:05:15,286 --> 00:05:19,376
HARPS at La Silla, and
confirmed in 2020 by ESPRESSO.

88
00:05:20,025 --> 00:05:21,985
The planet swings its star towards us and

89
00:05:22,215 --> 00:05:25,525
away every eleven days,
at about walking pace.

90
00:05:26,244 --> 00:05:30,424
That's walking pace, measured on a
star forty trillion kilometres away.

91
00:05:30,876 --> 00:05:32,996
And ESPRESSO is built
to measure even smaller

92
00:05:33,226 --> 00:05:36,736
motions, down to about
ten centimetres a second.

93
00:05:37,516 --> 00:05:40,636
That's roughly how fast the
Earth makes the Sun wobble.

94
00:05:42,796 --> 00:05:45,196
The second example doesn't
use visible light at all.

95
00:05:45,426 --> 00:05:50,016
ALMA, run by ESO and its
partners, is sixty-six

96
00:05:50,096 --> 00:05:53,376
antennas on a plateau
five thousand metres up.

97
00:05:53,923 --> 00:05:56,643
It picks up millimetre and
submillimetre waves, from

98
00:05:56,873 --> 00:06:00,843
cold gas and dust: the raw
material of stars and planets.

99
00:06:01,478 --> 00:06:05,098
And here, the dry air isn't
a bonus. It's essential.

100
00:06:05,918 --> 00:06:08,618
Water vapour absorbs exactly these waves.

101
00:06:09,378 --> 00:06:11,778
On a damp site, ALMA
would be nearly blind.

102
00:06:12,588 --> 00:06:15,208
It has photographed young
stars surrounded by discs of

103
00:06:15,438 --> 00:06:18,728
dust, with gaps where new
planets seem to be forming.

104
00:06:21,458 --> 00:06:23,398
So why build an even bigger one?

105
00:06:24,349 --> 00:06:27,489
Because the most interesting
things are often the faintest.

106
00:06:28,209 --> 00:06:30,429
Small planets next to bright stars.

107
00:06:30,549 --> 00:06:32,349
The very first galaxies.

108
00:06:33,409 --> 00:06:37,349
The Extremely Large Telescope,
the ELT, will collect nearly

109
00:06:37,579 --> 00:06:41,029
twenty times as much light
as one of the VLT's mirrors.

110
00:06:41,739 --> 00:06:44,579
A mirror that size can't
be made in one piece.

111
00:06:45,359 --> 00:06:46,979
It's made of seven
hundred and ninety-eight

112
00:06:47,059 --> 00:06:50,939
hexagonal segments, working
together as one surface.

113
00:06:51,429 --> 00:06:53,609
And it takes on the twinkling directly.

114
00:06:54,489 --> 00:06:58,169
More than five thousand actuators
reshape one of its mirrors, up

115
00:06:58,399 --> 00:07:02,409
to a thousand times a second, to
undo the blur of the atmosphere.

116
00:07:03,026 --> 00:07:04,566
It isn't finished yet.

117
00:07:05,186 --> 00:07:11,426
ESO plans first light in March 2029,
and the first science in December 2030.

118
00:07:13,927 --> 00:07:17,167
At this point, you might
reasonably ask: why bother?

119
00:07:17,487 --> 00:07:19,367
We have telescopes in space.

120
00:07:20,147 --> 00:07:22,547
No air, no clouds, no twinkling.

121
00:07:23,051 --> 00:07:27,611
Hubble has been up there since
1990, James Webb since 2021.

122
00:07:28,351 --> 00:07:31,471
And Webb, working mostly in
infrared, doesn't have to

123
00:07:31,701 --> 00:07:34,531
look through water vapour,
or the glow of warm air.

124
00:07:35,053 --> 00:07:38,093
But Webb's mirror is six and
a half metres across, and

125
00:07:38,323 --> 00:07:41,193
even that had to be folded
up to fit inside its rocket.

126
00:07:41,873 --> 00:07:46,113
A thirty-nine-metre mirror won't
fit inside any rocket we have.

127
00:07:46,471 --> 00:07:51,111
And on the ground, upgrading an instrument
takes a truck, not a space mission.

128
00:07:51,665 --> 00:07:53,505
So this isn't a contest of mirror size.

129
00:07:53,735 --> 00:07:56,925
Webb sees what the ground can't.

130
00:07:57,005 --> 00:07:59,625
And where the air lets
light through, the ELT

131
00:07:59,855 --> 00:08:02,585
will collect far more of
it, and see finer detail.

132
00:08:03,185 --> 00:08:06,465
Different questions, often
about the very same objects.

133
00:08:09,105 --> 00:08:12,325
And at the end of August
2026, another one joined them.

134
00:08:12,555 --> 00:08:15,685
NASA's Roman Space Telescope.

135
00:08:16,478 --> 00:08:20,158
It's heading for a point called L2,
one and a half million kilometres

136
00:08:20,238 --> 00:08:24,378
beyond the Earth, where it will
circle that point, just like Webb.

137
00:08:25,022 --> 00:08:27,682
Its mirror is the same size as Hubble's.

138
00:08:27,762 --> 00:08:31,702
But it sees about a hundred
times more sky in a single shot.

139
00:08:32,082 --> 00:08:36,162
Its job isn't to look
closer. It's to look wider.

140
00:08:36,836 --> 00:08:39,936
Roman might spot something strange
across a huge patch of sky.

141
00:08:40,166 --> 00:08:43,156
Webb might take a closer look.

142
00:08:43,716 --> 00:08:46,616
And the ELT might split
its light into a spectrum.

143
00:08:49,676 --> 00:08:53,476
So why is Europe building its
giant eyes in the Chilean desert?

144
00:08:54,209 --> 00:08:56,749
Because the sky it
wanted was in the south.

145
00:08:56,829 --> 00:09:00,609
And because Chile had one
of the clearest views of it.

146
00:09:01,534 --> 00:09:03,654
There's no single best telescope.

147
00:09:04,434 --> 00:09:09,054
Space or ground, big or wide, each one
sees a different part of the same story.

148
00:09:09,939 --> 00:09:12,299
And all of them depend on the same thing.

149
00:09:13,019 --> 00:09:16,779
A tiny amount of light, that crossed
the universe for billions of years...

150
00:09:17,261 --> 00:09:19,921
only to land on a mirror, built by people,

151
00:09:20,001 --> 00:09:22,271
in a desert on the
other side of the world.
